<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Cortona</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 310 BCE (approx.) · capital Cortona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Etruscan hill city above the Val di Chiana, known for its monumental tumuli and the Tabula Cortonensis, one of the longest Etruscan texts.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-cortona">Cortona</a>, Etruscan Curtun, sits high on a spur of Monte Sant'Egidio overlooking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/val-di-chiana">Val di Chiana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimeno</a>. Greek and Roman writers credited it with legendary origins, linking it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanus">Dardanus</a> or to Pelasgian settlers. Archaeologically it developed into a city in the 7th and 6th centuries BCE, when its first walls were built.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cortona's elite built the great 'Melone' tumuli at Sodo below the town, one with a monumental stepped altar decorated with sphinxes devouring warriors. Its bronzes include a famous hanging lamp with sixteen burners. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabula-cortonensis">Tabula Cortonensis</a>, a bronze tablet of about 200 BCE found in 1992, records a land transaction among named families and is a key source for the Etruscan language and law.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> names <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-cortona">Cortona</a> with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perusia">Perusia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arretium">Arretium</a> among the cities that made a thirty-year truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 310 BCE. It gradually became a Roman ally and later a municipium, overshadowed by Arretium. The Etruscan walls still underlie the medieval town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cortona.jpg" alt="Cortona" width="320" height="236"><figcaption>Image: Patrick Denker · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cortona-vista01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 310 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cortona</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Etruscan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Etruscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Curtun</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-555&amp;focus=cortona" aria-label="Cortona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4969 642 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cortona lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5289 898L5290 918M5289 898L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5290 918h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5289 898h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5289 898h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cortona. <a href="/atlas?year=-555&amp;focus=cortona">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

